WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.640
The gold standard to measure your VO2 max

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:05.700
is going to be in a laboratory
with a metabolic cart.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.090
You can get this stuff
in various equations.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.010
You can use any of your
fitness technologies.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.840
I will strongly encourage you though

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.030
that if you care about this number,

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:16.260
if it's possible,

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:17.910
and it may not be,

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:19.620
spend a couple of hundred dollars

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.230
and get this actually
tested in the laboratory.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:24.420
The data are quite clear, at this point.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.180
I have not yet seen really any standard

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:29.160
over the counter fitness technology

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:31.620
that gets an accurate
number of your VO2 max

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.090
when the number gets high

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:36.150
and particularly for people
who are already fairly fit.

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.730
If you're VO2 max is really low,

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:40.020
it might give you a decent number.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.330
But for folks that are kind
of moderate to highly trained,

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:43.560
it's just really challenging

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.720
to get an appropriate
estimate from a watch

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:47.580
or a ring or things like that.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.020
Perhaps, those will improve in the future.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:50.790
In fact, I quite expect them to.

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:52.140
But as of now,

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:53.850
the margin of error is a
little bit too high for me

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:57.030
to be comfortable with when
you care about accuracy.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:00.030
If you're trying to just get
a global sense, they're fine.

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:00.930
In fact, if you want to do that,

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:01.980
you can use any number

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.950
of absolutely free estimate equations.

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:06.060
Examples of this would include

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:07.860
something like a two-minute step test

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.900
where you would take your heart rate,

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:13.590
step up and down on a very
small box, say 12 inches or so.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:15.930
You do that continuously for two minutes

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:17.700
and then measure your
heart rate at the end.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.610
You can enter that score into an equation

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.380
and get an estimate of your VO2 max.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.980
Those are scientifically
validated methodologies.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:27.450
The 12-minute test ends up being

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:29.730
something like a mile and a half run.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.140
So you could simply, in fact,

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:32.580
you could do this if you'd like, as well.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.860
Run a mile and a half as fast as you can.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:36.450
Take that time.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.820
Enter it into equation
and get an estimate.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.160
If you have that time as
well as your heart rate,

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:42.240
you can enter it in as well

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.160
and get a more accurate picture.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:45.780
And so again, all of these are close.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.390
We call these sub-maximal estimates

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:49.260
because they are that,

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:50.810
they're not the direct measure.

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:53.280
So I would encourage you, again,

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.920
if at all possible to
actually go into a laboratory

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.513
and get this measured.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.140
In addition, if you do
something like that,

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:02.940
you can get a bunch of other metrics

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:05.910
you can't get with some of
these estimated equations,

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:07.860
like what percentage of fat

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:09.600
versus carbohydrate you're using,

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.910
your anaerobic and lactate thresholds,

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:12.990
your maximum ventilation

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:15.330
so how much total air
you can bring in and out,

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:17.970
and a bunch of other stuff that
we talked about on the show

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:19.710
and we'll get into in a second.

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:22.200
Again, if you don't have access
to any of that, that's fine.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.090
Use any of those other free

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:25.710
or extremely low cost options

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:27.540
and you'll get yourself pretty close.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:30.540
Our next "I" is interpretation.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:33.120
So sticking with heart rate and VO2 max,

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:35.190
as I mentioned a little bit earlier,

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:38.490
resting heart rate will go
down as you improve fitness,

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:42.030
but your maximum heart rate
will not really change.

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:44.400
In fact, if anything, it will be reduced

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.230
because you're a little bit older.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.570
And so to state it one more time,

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:49.920
there's no real association

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:51.360
between highly fit people

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.603
and their maximum heart
rate, and unfit people.

00:02:53.603 --> 00:02:54.990
So it's not a metric

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:56.970
that we should be overly
concerned about of,

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:59.220
you know, where is your
maximum heart rate.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.620
It is relevant to, again,
your stroke volume,

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:04.200
but the heart rate itself is
not going to tell you that much.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.360
So not something to be concerned about.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.160
Your resting heart rate though,

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:12.900
or your heart rate at any given
intensity is very important.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:13.920
So if you're going to be running,

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:15.570
say at a standard pace,

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:18.420
say seven miles per hour

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.490
and your heart rate at
seven miles per hour

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:22.530
was 150 beats per minute

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.030
and now a couple of months later,

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:26.880
your heart rate at the exact same speed

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:28.980
is now 115 beats per minute.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.380
That would represent a
significant improvement

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:32.670
in cardiovascular fitness.

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:33.990
Stroke volume would be much higher.

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:34.890
And because of that,

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:36.990
that allows your heart rate to come down.

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:39.494
Resting heart rate is a similar story.

00:03:39.494 --> 00:03:40.560
I'm giving you a little
bit of context here.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:41.940
And the reason this is top of mind

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:44.370
is because there's a hummingbird
that lives in my backyard

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:47.220
and my wife and kids look
at it every single day

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:48.270
and they get so excited.

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:50.550
They call her mom's little helper.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:52.050
They named it Squirt, actually.

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:53.010
And so they get really excited

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:54.630
when this little hummingbird
is flying back there

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:56.400
'cause it's super fast.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:58.230
Hummingbirds have a resting heart rate

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:01.200
of something like 1200 beats per minute.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:02.670
It's absolutely insane.

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:05.640
So you're talking about
putting an order of magnitude

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.640
on top of almost a human
maximum heart rate.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.490
You contrast that to a
larger animal like a giraffe,

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:13.110
an elephant or a blue whale

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:14.340
and you're talking about a heart rate

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:16.830
of something like five
to six beats per minute.

00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:19.890
And so humans, of course,
are somewhere in between.

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.630
If you were technically to Google this,

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:23.100
you might see something like

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:26.580
a normal resting heart rate
is 60 to 100 beats per minute.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:27.750
I'm here to tell you,

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:29.670
I cannot fathom a situation

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:30.900
in which somebody's resting heart rate

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.360
is over 80 beats per minute
and they are healthy.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:34.710
And I absolutely would not think that

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:35.790
that would happen to somebody

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:37.470
who's performing at their maximum.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.900
In fact, I even will tell you,

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:41.640
this is off the record,

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:42.720
this is not the science,

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:45.120
this is me and my professional opinion.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:48.150
Even a resting a heart rate of 60 or so,

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:51.060
particularly in a man is...

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:52.140
That's catching my eye.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:53.910
I would like to see most folks probably

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:56.040
in the forties to fifties,

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.150
somewhere in the range.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:04:59.070
Again, you might be fine at 60,

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:00.840
but getting a much above 60

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:02.550
is quite a bit high.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:03.720
I also personally tell you,

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:05.370
I've worked with plenty of athletes,

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:07.140
specifically in this case,

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:08.760
several UFC fighters

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:11.610
whose resting heart rates
were in the low forties,

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:12.780
if not high thirties.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:15.960
So 37, 38, 40, things like this.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:17.850
And so that's the lowest
I have personally seen.

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:19.890
That said, there are classic stories

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:21.480
of plenty of endurance athletes.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:22.860
You're talking about elite cyclists

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:25.560
and cross country skiers and
marathon runners and such

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:27.210
who are in the low thirties.

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:29.070
Lowest I'm aware of

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:32.130
is the legendary cyclist, Miguel Indurain,

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:34.590
having a resting heart rate
of 28 beats per minute.

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:35.550
As far as I can tell,

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:36.870
that's the lowest ever reported

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:37.920
in the scientific literature.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:41.490
Though please, if you've
seen any lower, let me know.

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:44.280
I'm sure there are plenty
of stories of anecdotes

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:47.070
of people in personal
training records and stuff

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:47.903
who think they're lower.

00:05:47.903 --> 00:05:50.220
But if you've ever seen anything
verified scientifically,

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:51.420
I would love to see that.

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:54.180
Interpreting your VO2 max is
more interesting in my opinion

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:56.220
'cause there's a lot of components to it.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:57.870
And so in order to truly understand this,

00:05:57.870 --> 00:06:01.410
let's talk about how we
calculate VO2 max to begin with.

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:03.300
The easiest way in my opinion

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:06.690
is to think about VO2 max
equals your cardiac output

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:11.130
multiplied by what's called
your AVO2 difference.

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:12.180
Now, as I've stated,

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:15.390
cardiac output is simply
your stroke volume

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:16.710
multiplied by your heart rate.

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:18.210
So if we were to combine
this entire thing,

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:20.490
we would say your heart rate

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:22.320
multiplied by your stroke volume,

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:23.430
which says, okay,

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:25.980
how much blood am I getting out per pump?

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:27.870
How many pumps am I getting?

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:31.050
And I multiply that by what's
called your AVO2 difference.

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:33.450
Now the A stands for arterial,

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:35.250
V stands for venous,

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:37.140
and the O2 is oxygen.

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:38.490
So what literally this means

00:06:38.490 --> 00:06:41.640
is what's the difference
in oxygen concentrations

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:46.260
between the arterial
side and the venous side?

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:49.650
Remember, arteries generally
go away from your heart,

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:51.840
which means they're going
to exercising tissue,

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:54.210
and veins come back.

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:55.620
And so what we're really
looking at is saying,

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:56.880
okay, how much blood,

00:06:56.880 --> 00:06:59.340
how much oxygen's in the blood
when it leaves the heart,

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:00.990
this is going to be the
highest concentration

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:02.133
of oxygen possible,

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:05.250
and how much is in the
blood when it comes back

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:07.320
having passed through muscle?

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:08.850
This then directly tells you

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:13.020
how much oxygen your tissue
extracted in the process.

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:14.970
So to give you some numbers here,

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:15.810
to make this easy,

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:16.740
these are not accurate,

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:19.200
just representing the math here.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:22.560
If you had a hundred molecules of oxygen

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:24.030
that left your heart

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:27.630
and went into your quadriceps,

00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:29.400
and then once it's gone
through the capillaries

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:32.280
that surround all the muscles
and tissues and fibers

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:33.810
in your quadriceps

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:35.100
and it went in as a hundred

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:37.200
and then it came back out the other side

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:40.620
and went back to the heart
and lungs to be re-oxygenated,

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:42.210
well, if it went in at a hundred

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:44.460
and came out as 75,

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:46.980
the difference between the
arterial and the venous side

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:49.980
is a hundred minus 75,

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:52.350
which would give you a score of 25.

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:54.150
Now what that means, and again,

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:57.510
I'm using those round
numbers to make this simple.

00:07:57.510 --> 00:07:59.970
You've extracted now 25% of the oxygen

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:00.990
that came in your system.

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:02.820
You only got 25% of it out.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:04.230
That's not a very good score.

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:06.690
You want that number,
because it's a multiplier,

00:08:06.690 --> 00:08:08.280
to be as high as possible.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:11.010
So if a hundred goes in, I
don't want 75 coming out,

00:08:11.010 --> 00:08:13.080
I want zero coming out.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:15.090
Let's say maybe you got 10 out.

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:17.490
So now a hundred went in, 10 came out.

00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:19.680
You extracted 90% of the oxygen

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:21.570
that was available to you

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:23.280
and got to bring it into muscle

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:26.220
and use it for everything
we talked about earlier.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:28.350
And so now your AVO2 difference is 90,

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:29.580
much higher than 25.

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:31.860
So we get to multiply that
by our cardiac output,

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:35.010
which brings our VO2 max even higher.

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:36.690
The easiest way to think about VO2 max

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:39.000
is to use what's called
the thick equation.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:41.580
VO2 max, according to the thick equation

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:43.020
is your heart rate,

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:45.240
in other words, how many beats per minute,

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:46.620
multiplied by your stroke volume,

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:48.330
how much blood coming out per pump,

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:51.690
multiplied by what's called
your AVO2 difference.

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:52.980
To be perfectly honest with you,

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:54.360
the AVO2 difference numbers

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:56.970
are difficult to convey over audio only.

00:08:56.970 --> 00:08:58.320
So I'm going to spend most of our time

00:08:58.320 --> 00:08:59.910
on the other side of the equation.

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:02.730
When it comes time to
interpreting your VO2 max numbers,

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:05.070
there's a lot of charts
and papers you can use.

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:07.363
We'll provide some of those
in the show notes for you.

00:09:07.363 --> 00:09:09.240
I want to give you some numbers though

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:10.710
to give you rough context.

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:12.450
Typically, we think about VO2 max

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:13.890
in what's called a relative term.

00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:15.720
Now scientists and people

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:16.620
that are more advanced in this field

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:19.680
might like to use the absolute versions.

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:21.630
Depending on the scenario,
that might make more sense.

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:23.280
But for now, let's just
stick to the relative.

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:24.450
What that means is

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:27.030
how many milliliters of oxygen

00:09:27.030 --> 00:09:28.170
are you using

00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:30.360
per kilogram of body weight

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:31.530
per minute?

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:32.790
So you'll see them expressed

00:09:32.790 --> 00:09:36.360
as things like your VO2
max is 50 milliliters

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:37.440
per kilogram

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:38.273
per minute.

00:09:38.273 --> 00:09:39.960
So 50 milliliters of oxygen

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:41.640
per kilogram of body weight

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:42.473
per minute.

00:09:42.473 --> 00:09:43.770
You can kind of think about VO2 max

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:46.470
almost on a scale of zero to 100.

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:48.930
And so the average person
that would kind of say,

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:50.580
walk out of my classroom

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:53.100
somewhat moderately
trained, male or female,

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:56.250
that's you know, in the
neighborhood of 170 pounds

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:58.500
or 70 kilos, something like that,

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:00.060
is probably going to
have a VO2 max around,

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:03.960
you know, 35 to 45 milliliters
per kilogram per minute.

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:05.250
Something in that range.

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:09.120
If you fall below 18 milliliters
per kilogram per minute,

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:11.040
you're probably crossing
below the threshold

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:12.360
of what we call independence.

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:15.030
For women, that's about 15 or 16,

00:10:15.030 --> 00:10:17.610
which means it's very
difficult to live independently

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:18.840
and by yourself

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:20.190
because your fitness is so low

00:10:20.190 --> 00:10:21.570
going through basic activities

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:23.910
of daily living become challenging.

00:10:23.910 --> 00:10:24.930
So what I'd like to share with you

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:27.180
is not only the normative values,

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:29.310
but also the highest we've ever seen

00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:33.540
in terms of VO2 max, cardiac
output and stroke volume.

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:35.850
Now there's so much data on VO2 max,

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:38.760
we can actually break this down by age

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:40.020
and by sex,

00:10:40.020 --> 00:10:41.460
in really specific numbers.

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:44.430
So if you know your exact age,

00:10:44.430 --> 00:10:46.200
you can go ahead and look
these up in these charts.

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:47.190
But I'll give you a couple

00:10:47.190 --> 00:10:49.410
again just to give you a ballpark.

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:52.020
Let's say you were a female

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:54.480
between 40 and 49-years-old

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:56.970
and your VO2 max was 28.

00:10:56.970 --> 00:11:00.720
That would put you as what we
consider to be below average.

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:03.120
If you wanted to go from
below average to average,

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:05.040
you'd have to go into the 32

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:07.590
to 36 milliliters per kilogram range.

00:11:07.590 --> 00:11:09.150
If you wanted to go all the way to elite,

00:11:09.150 --> 00:11:10.530
tell me what's the best ever,

00:11:10.530 --> 00:11:12.630
you'd be needing to surpass the mark

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:15.900
of 47 milliliters per kilogram per minute.

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:17.310
Any of you that are maybe older,

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:18.990
let's go ahead and jump way down.

00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:22.020
Let's say you are 71-years-old,

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:24.240
again, talking about a
71-year-old female here.

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:26.250
Below 18, you know,

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:27.840
you're still above that
line of independence,

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:29.730
but it would still be considered low.

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:32.790
You would really want to be
looking at something like 22 to 24

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:34.770
to be considered above average

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:38.550
and then really over 36
to be considered elite.

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:42.120
Now I know my friend, Peter
Attia, likes to tell people

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:44.310
he wants them to be considered elite

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:47.100
in at least the decade
before their actual age,

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:49.080
if not two decades before.

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:50.940
And that's a phenomenal
way to think about it.

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:52.440
So just as that example,

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:54.900
if you were that incredibly ambitious

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:57.000
and vigorous 71-year-old,

00:11:57.000 --> 00:11:59.460
elite for you would be 36 milliliters

00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:01.020
per kilogram per minute.

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:05.130
The decade before, again
60 to 69, that would be 40.

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:06.930
But if you wanted to be that extra person

00:12:06.930 --> 00:12:09.330
to get that double gold stamp from Peter,

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:10.980
you'd want to be above 46.

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:14.490
So the difference was going from 36 to 46

00:12:14.490 --> 00:12:16.470
would give you that elite category

00:12:16.470 --> 00:12:18.870
for two decades younger than you.

00:12:18.870 --> 00:12:20.970
For the men, it's a similar story.

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:22.950
You just add a little
bit of the numbers to it.

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:27.213
So a male who is say
between 50 and 59-years-old,

00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:30.150
above average would be 36

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:32.550
to 40 milliliters per kilogram per minute.

00:12:32.550 --> 00:12:34.620
And you'd need to be above 50

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:37.050
to be considered elite at that age.

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:38.550
Now, in my personal opinion,

00:12:38.550 --> 00:12:40.860
I kind of like to say there's
no excuse to be under 50

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:42.660
unless you're over 50,

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:43.920
meaning you have no reason

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:46.080
to be having a VO2 max of lower than 50

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:47.700
unless you're over 50-years-old.

00:12:47.700 --> 00:12:49.170
And even then, 50 to 60,

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:52.290
I don't want you anywhere near below 50.

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:54.990
So that's a nice number to go after.

00:12:54.990 --> 00:12:57.780
We'll also talk about in a
second how changeable that is

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:00.840
and how much it responds to
various types of training.

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:02.130
And so it's going to give
you a little bit of hope.

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:04.290
You have some room to move there

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:05.880
and it will respond to your training.

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:07.260
And so there's some light
at the end of the tunnel.

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:09.120
If you're looking at those numbers

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:10.980
and you've had a VO2 max done recently

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:11.813
and you're thinking,

00:13:11.813 --> 00:13:15.060
"Oh my gosh, I am way below
what Andy said there,"

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:16.050
that's fine.

00:13:16.050 --> 00:13:19.608
You still have within your
capabilities to change that.

00:13:19.608 --> 00:13:22.358
[music]

